  "How many neighbors make a decision?": "<style>@media(min-width:801px) and (max-height:780px){.public-quorum .sim canvas{height:28vh;min-height:200px}.public-quorum>.footnote{font-size:15px;margin-top:12px}}</style><div class=\"public-quorum\"><div class=\"sim-heading\"><h2>How many neighbors<br>make a decision?</h2><p class=\"lead\">A colony needs a new home.</p></div><div class=\"sim\"><canvas id=\"quorumCanvas\" width=\"1000\" height=\"300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Scouts inspect two candidate nests. Reaching a local threshold starts transport.\">Scouts compare two nests. Read their numbers and the decision below.</canvas><div class=\"controls\"><button id=\"quorumPlay\">Start scouts</button><button id=\"quorumStep\">One assessment</button><button id=\"quorumReset\">New search</button><label for=\"threshold\">Threshold <output id=\"thresholdValue\">8</output><input id=\"threshold\" type=\"range\" min=\"3\" max=\"12\" value=\"8\"></label></div><p class=\"sim-status\" id=\"quorumStatus\">Near / exposed: 0 \u00b7 Far / sheltered: 0 \u00b7 Searching</p></div><p class=\"footnote\">Toy model inspired by <i>Temnothorax</i> nest choice. Counts and thresholds are invented for teaching; not a local-species claim.</p><p class=\"footnote\"><a href=\"https://pratt.lab.asu.edu/videos\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Watch actual nest-search recruitment &amp; nestmate transport \u2197</a> \u00b7 Stephen Pratt / Arizona State University \u00b7 internet needed</p></div>",
  "Living ladders": "<div class=\"film-layout\"><div><h2 id=\"title-10\">Living ladders</h2><p class=\"lead\">Some ants become<br>the structure.</p><p class=\"watch-cue\">Watch the lower panel.<br>Which ants stop moving?</p></div><figure><video id=\"scaffoldVideo\" controls muted playsinline preload=\"none\" aria-label=\"Research timelapse of Eciton burchellii ants assembling scaffolds at 50 and 90 degree slopes\"><source src=\"https://www.uni-konstanz.de/fileadmin/pi/fileserver/2021_EXTRA/learning_about_system_stability_from_ants_image_matthew_lutz_video_matthew_lutz_chris_reid_simon_garnier_and_iain_couzin.mp4\" type=\"video/mp4\"><track kind=\"captions\" src=\"site/ants/scaffolds.vtt\" srclang=\"en\" label=\"English description\" default></video><figcaption>Internet needed \u00b7 university-hosted film<br>15-second research timelapse \u00b7 40\u00d7 speed \u00b7 <i>Eciton burchellii</i><br><a href=\"https://www.eurekalert.org/news-releases/722631\">Lutz, Reid, Garnier & Couzin / University of Konstanz</a></figcaption></figure></div><p class=\"footnote\">Researchers call these \u201cscaffolds.\u201d Stationary ants provide footholds on a slope. A wider-world example.</p>",
